Portability fixes for HarmonyOS PC (OHOS/aarch64, cmake 4.1, clang 15).

1. kpty.cpp relied on transitive Qt includes for QFile/QFileInfo, which
   this Qt5 configuration does not provide; include them directly.
2. QString::sprintf and the unqualified endl are gone from that build;
   use QString::asprintf and Qt::endl.
3. KPty::login()/logout() are compiled out entirely when the platform has
   neither the utmpx API (HAVE_UTMPX) nor login()/logout() (HAVE_LOGIN),
   which is the only situation in which upstream would fall back to the
   legacy updwtmp()/getutline() bookkeeping. HarmonyOS is such a platform
   and its stub utmp.h declares neither symbol. Enclosed with #if/#else so
   the code is excluded at compile time; platforms with a real utmp keep
   upstream behaviour unchanged.
4. KPtyDevicePrivate::_k_canRead() reserved the read buffer from an
   ioctl(TIOCINQ) queue length on the pty master. HarmonyOS denies that ioctl
   (and FIONREAD, same request number) with EACCES on the master while the
   slave answers it, so the guarded read never ran, the zero byte count was
   taken for EOF, and the read notifier was switched off for good - all five
   upstream KPtyProcessTest cases then failed on a pty that never delivered a
   byte. Fall back to reading a chunk when the ioctl fails; the master is
   O_NONBLOCK, so the read returns what is queued, and the unreserve() call
   releases the surplus. Where the ioctl works nothing changes, including the
   closed-slave case that legitimately reads zero bytes and reports readEof.

5. The same function has to report end-of-file when the slave side closes.
   Linux spells that as EIO from read() on the master, and upstream never sees
   it because its ioctl reports a zero-length queue, so the zero-length read
   returns 0 and the EOF branch runs. With the ioctl unavailable the read is a
   real one and returns -1/EIO instead, which the error branch would swallow -
   the readEof signal would never fire and test_pty_signals would lose its
   trailing marker. EIO is therefore mapped to the same end-of-file; any other
   errno still goes to the error branch.

